<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dmitry Donskoy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1350">1350</a>–<a href="/years/1389">1389</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Dmitry Ivanovich</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dmitry Donskoy was Grand Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> from 1359 and of Vladimir from 1362. He built the first stone Kremlin in Moscow and made Moscow the leading Russian principality over Tver and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryazan">Ryazan</a>. His victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamai">Mamai</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kulikovo">Battle of Kulikovo</a> in 1380, on the Don, gave him his name, though Moscow was sacked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokhtamysh">Tokhtamysh</a> in 1382.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dmitry-donskoy.jpg" alt="Dmitry Donskoy" width="320" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown. Died over 100 years ago · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dmitri_Donskoy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The army's weakness showed during the Great Troubles: in 1380 the warlord Mamai was defeated by Grand Prince <b>Dmitry of Moscow</b> at the Battle of Kulikovo, although Khan Tokhtamysh sacked Moscow two years later.</blockquote><a href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Dmitry Donskoy</b> replaced the oak walls of the Kremlin with white limestone in 1367–1368, and the new fortress withstood sieges by Algirdas of Lithuania in 1368 and 1370.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>From the fourteenth century Moscow’s princes, holding the title of grand prince of Vladimir, claimed the right to be Novgorod’s prince and demanded tribute; <b>Dmitry Donskoy</b> forced the city to pay a large indemnity in 1386.</blockquote><a href="/empire/novgorod-republic">Novgorod Republic · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Dmitry Konstantinovich held the grand-princely title of Vladimir from 1360 to 1363 before giving way to <b>Dmitry Donskoy</b>, who married his daughter Evdokia.</blockquote><a href="/state/grand-principality-of-nizhny-novgorod-suzdal">Grand Principality of Nizhny Novgorod-Suzdal</a></li><li><blockquote>Oleg Ivanovich, who ruled for most of 1350 to 1402, stood up to <b>Dmitry Donskoy</b> and kept out of the Battle of Kulikovo.</blockquote><a href="/state/grand-principality-of-ryazan">Grand Principality of Ryazan</a></li><li><blockquote>Prince Fyodor Romanovich and his son were killed fighting for <b>Dmitry Donskoy</b> at Kulikovo in 1380.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-beloozero">Principality of Beloozero</a></li></ul></section>
